# The fixture filesystem fsck's b6sim cases repair -- task C4d.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT ../../df/test/fsimg.img.  ../../TODO.md says C4d should copy df's fixture
# rather than build a second, and for `dd' (C4b) that was right: dd does not care what is
# in the blocks it moves.  fsck does.  df's fixture has no lost+found, no hard link, no
# device node, no indirect block and nothing nested below depth 1, so the reconnect path,
# the link-count path, ftypeok()'s device arms and iblock() are all unreachable from it --
# and it cannot be extended, because cmd/df/test/image.expected asserts a literal free
# count that any addition would move.  So there are two fixtures, and the `clean' case
# runs against DF's, which makes the pair a cross-check rather than a duplication: two
# independently built filesystems that must both come out clean, and a free count fsck
# arrives at by walking the list that must equal the one df prints.
#
# WHAT EACH ENTRY IS FOR.  Every one of these is reached by a damage case; nothing here is
# decoration.
#
#   /lost+found      the reconnect target.  An ORDINARY EMPTY DIRECTORY, which is the
#                    interesting part: fsck.1m says one must first create files in it and
#                    delete them again, and that is not true here.  linkup() rounds
#                    di_size up to a whole block and the rest of the block is zeros --
#                    b6fsutil's dir.cpp starts from `Block b{}', the kernel's alloc() calls
#                    clrbuf() -- and a block holds DIRPB (128) entries, so a fresh
#                    lost+found has 126 empty slots.  fsck.1m is corrected rather than
#                    carried.
#   /big             21,504 bytes = 7 blocks, which is one MORE than the six direct
#                    addresses (NADDR 8, NLEVEL 2), so it has a single indirect block and
#                    iblock() runs.  df's fixture stops deliberately at three blocks.
#   /etc/passwd      a file with a hard link, so a link count above 1 exists for the
#                    `linkcnt' case to be wrong about.
#   /etc/passwd.bak  that link.
#   /dev/tty         a device node, so ckinode()'s SPECIAL arm is taken -- di_addr[0] IS
#                    the device number there, and walking it as a block number is the bug
#                    cmd/fsutil/check.cpp has a test of its own for.
#   /d/e/f           three levels, so descend() recurses and a `..' can be wrong two deep.
#
# 200 blocks, and a fixed timestamp, for ../../df/test/fsimg.manifest's reasons: the image
# must be byte-reproducible or a free count is not an expectation.  The `source' paths
# resolve against b6fsutil's working directory, which is this test's build directory.

default
owner 0
group 0
dirmode 0755
filemode 0644

dir /lost+found
dir /etc
dir /d
dir /d/e

file /big
source fsckimg.d/big

file /small
owner 201
source fsckimg.d/small

file /etc/passwd
source fsckimg.d/small

link /etc/passwd.bak
target /etc/passwd

file /d/e/f
owner 202
source fsckimg.d/small

cdev /dev/tty
major 5
minor 0
mode 0620
